A||N||5||FieroSTETZ||a lot of valve noise from new engine||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||05-24-2001||02:56 AM||||i have about 1600 miles on my new engine, running standard 10w40 oil (switching to 5w30 or 10w30 mobil 1 after 2000 miles) and as the engine gets more miles, i notice that there is a lot of clacking from the engine, and it stays constant with engine rpm - this is actual clacking, not exhaust noise. what should i do? is it normal for everything to loosen up? if so, how long before i should tighten it up?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-24-2001||03:11 AM||||What kind of engine is it?  Is it a V6?<P>Clacking could be excessive valve train lash.  The 2.8L V6 engine has an adjustment procedure for lash.  There's no reason why you cannot check the valve lash adjustment after 1600 miles.  Just get a book and follow the directions.<P>If you don't have a book, then email me and I will transcribe them for you.  You should still get a book!<P>If you have an Iron Duke, then there may be a problem with your new engine (Since these engines are not manufactured anymore, I bet new means rebuilt).   Did you use new lifters and cam?  Are the pushrods known to be straight and free of obstructions? (they are the oil path for the upper valve train)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroSTETZ||05-24-2001||12:47 PM||||thats what i thought it was, just wanted a second opinion - is there actually a way to remove the valve covers without removing the intake plenum? the books seem to think so but i can't seem to do it - thanks for the response!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.shawnrocks.com/SHAWN.GIF"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||stsmithgt||05-24-2001||06:15 PM||||I had an auto shop teacher in high school that had a little game he'd play.  If you said you "can't" do something, he would flunk you and boot you out of the shop right then and there.  People learn fast how to rephrase there words. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  He usually cut the guy off when he thought he might say can't to give him a chance, but some didn't catch the hint. Now when ever someone says "can't" I'm unable at this time to keep his little game out of my head.(brainwashed!) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||stsmithgt||05-24-2001||06:18 PM||||I had an auto shop teacher in high school that had a little game he'd play.  If you said you "can't" do something, he would flunk you and boot you out of the shop right then and there.  People learn fast how to rephrase there words. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  He usually cut the guy off when he thought he might say can't to give him a chance, but some didn't catch the hint. Now when ever someone says "can't" I'm unable at this time to keep his little game out of my head.(brainwashed!) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>DAMN! and that was over 20 years ago!<P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-24-2001||09:56 PM||||The valve cover will come off an Iron Duke after you remove the EGR valve.  It looks like you can't do it, but a craftsman 13mm wrench will get in there just well enough to do the job.  <P>You will need to remove the air cleaner assembly and the PCV pipe from valve cover to TBI.  Then, the EGR valve sits just between valve cover and TBI.  Get a new gasket, a gasket scraper, and some copper gasket sealant (little can with brush) before you re-install the EGR.  If it leaks, you will hear a ticking sound that could be mistaken for a valve train problem.<P>Dukes have noisey valves, but this usually does not get to be loud until the valve train or bottom end is pretty worn.  I pulled a crank from a Duke that made bottom end "loose bolts in the crankcase" noises that could be heard from 10 feet away.  The bearings were shot, but the crank did not need a re-grind.  <P>My point?  Iron Dukes can make bad noises that sound like a "mechanical cacauphony" when they are still repairable.  <P>Now, tell me about your new engine.  What specifically was done?  Is it a "Kragen Motor"?  (local discount autoparts store). <P>My freshly rebuilt Duke is sitting on the engine stand ready to be re-installed.  It should have about 110 HP power output.  This is almost a 25% gain over stock. Email me if you are interested in the details of this build.   ||||reg||1
